const e={language:"Avestan",intro:"Avestan is the language of the Zoroastrian scriptures and of nothing else — no contract, no chronicle, no letter, no inscription survives in it, and the name of the language is a modern coinage taken from the name of the book. It is an Old Iranian tongue standing so close to the Sanskrit of the Rigveda that lines can be carried from one into the other by regular sound-law alone: Avestan haoma is Vedic soma, yasna is yajña, aṣ̌a is ṛta, and the priest who pours the offering is zaotar on one side of the divide and hotar on the other. It survives in two stages that are centuries apart — Old Avestan, the language of the seventeen Gathas ascribed to Zarathustra, of the prose Yasna Haptaŋhāiti, and of four short prayers; and Younger Avestan, in which the rest of the corpus stands, the Yashts to the yazatas, the Visperad, the Videvdad, and the greater part of the seventy-two chapters of the Yasna. For something on the order of a millennium and a half none of it was written down at all. It was carried in the mouths of priests, memorised in a form fixed by metre and by the exactness of the rite, and the Zoroastrian tradition's own explanation of why nothing older survives — an archetype written in gold ink on prepared ox-hides and burned by Alexander — is told in the Ardā Wirāz Nāmag and in the fourth book of the Dēnkard, where modern scholarship reads it as an aetiology for a loss rather than a record of one. The alphabet in which the Avesta now stands was made for it, in the Sasanian period, by men who could hear what was being recited and did not intend it to drift: more than fifty letters, sixteen of them vowels, distinguishing a long schwa from a short one and a released final t from an unreleased one, distinctions no reader who knew the language would ever have needed. Everything about that alphabet says that its makers were recording a performance rather than a text. And still nothing survives in it from before the thirteenth century: the oldest dated Avestan manuscripts are Indian and Iranian copies of the 1280s and 1320s, so that between the invention of a script designed to stop a liturgy changing and the earliest book written in it there lies a gap of seven hundred years filled by inference. The tradition it records is not closed. The Yasna is performed today in Iran and in India, in Avestan, by priests who learn the text by heart before they can read it — which is, in the end, the older of the two technologies at work.",scriptNote:"The Avestan alphabet runs right to left in unjoined letters, each word divided from the next by a single dot set on or above the line, and the same dot separates the members of a compound, so that a page of Avestan is punctuated at every word-boundary in a way no other script of the region attempted. Its base is the Book Pahlavi script of the Sasanian chancery, and the reason it exists is the deficiency of that base: Book Pahlavi had let so many originally distinct Aramaic letters fall together that a single shape could be read three or four ways, and words were identified by memory and context rather than by their spelling. The Avestan alphabet un-merges those shapes and then goes far beyond them, adding letters until every phonetic distinction a reciting priest could make had a sign of its own — four velar fricatives where Pahlavi had one usable letter, three velar nasals, four sibilants beyond s and z, two letters for y, a separate letter for the m of the cluster hm, and a t̰ used only where the sound falls unreleased at the end of a word. The vowels are the greater innovation: no script of the Aramaic family had written vowels as full letters standing in the line, and the model is usually taken to have been Greek, which the Sasanian court knew. Sixteen vowel letters keep eight long-short pairs apart, nasal vowels among them. The alphabet is counted at fifty-three letters in the standard reconstruction, thirty-seven consonants and sixteen vowels; Unicode encodes fifty-four, the extra being le, a sign for a sound Avestan does not possess, added later for Pazand — Middle Persian written in Avestan characters precisely to escape the ambiguity of Pahlavi — and for foreign words. The letters have no traditional names and no name-meanings: being invented for sounds rather than inherited from pictures of oxen and doors, they are called by their sounds, and the names given below (ke, xe, ge, and the rest) are the modern convention adopted for the encoding. There are no numerals; the block contains no digits, and manuscripts number their chapters in the numeral habits of the surrounding written culture. Two conventions of the standard transliteration should be read as descriptions of the page rather than as scholarly ornament: ii and uu each stand for two written vowel letters where an English speaker would write y and w, so mainiiu, vairiia, hauruuatāt̰ and frauuaṣ̌i are spelt in the manuscripts exactly as they are printed here. The date of the invention is argued. A fourth-century origin has been urged in connection with the priest Ādurbād ī Mahraspandān under Shapur II, a sixth-century one under Khosrow I; Karl Hoffmann's reconstruction of a single Sasanian archetype standing behind every surviving manuscript does not by itself fix the century, and Alberto Cantera has argued that what the manuscripts actually transmit are ritual books for use rather than an edition of a text, which makes the archetype as usually drawn look tidier than the evidence.",letters:[{glyph:"𐬀",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER A",name:"a",translit:"a",pronunciation:"Short a as in 'cup'.",meaning:"Short a. The Avestan letters carry no name-meanings of the kind aleph and beth have; being invented for sounds, they are called by them.",note:"The commonest letter in the corpus and the one the alphabet is built outward from. A final short a is metrically real in the Gathas, and their verse can be scanned only because the script records it — where Book Pahlavi would have left an ending to be supplied by a reader who already knew the word."},{glyph:"𐬁",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER AA",name:"aa",translit:"ā",pronunciation:"Long a as in 'father'.",meaning:"Long ā.",note:"Length is phonemic in Avestan and the alphabet never leaves it to be guessed. This is the first of eight long-short vowel pairs kept apart by separate letters rather than by marks, and the whole case system of the language depends on the distinction being visible."},{glyph:"𐬂",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER AO",name:"ao",translit:"å",pronunciation:"A rounded long a, between the a of 'father' and the aw of 'law'.",meaning:"Rounded long a.",note:"This is the vowel that ends the nominative mazdå, where the vocative has plain ā — so that the name of God is spelt with one letter when he is spoken of and another when he is spoken to. Neither Pahlavi nor any Aramaic-derived script of the region could have shown the difference."},{glyph:"𐬃",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER AAO",name:"aao",translit:"ā̊",pronunciation:"The long counterpart of å.",meaning:"Long rounded a.",note:"One of the rarest signs in the alphabet, made for a handful of occurrences. A letter cut for a sound that will be needed a few dozen times is evidence about intent: the designers were not economising, they were transcribing a recitation, and a sound heard rarely still had to be written."},{glyph:"𐬄",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER AN",name:"an",translit:"ą",pronunciation:"A nasalised a, as in French 'an'.",meaning:"Nasalised a.",note:"A nasal vowel given a letter of its own rather than a mark set over another letter — the ą of mąθra, the potent utterance. Nasalisation is among the distinctions the Pahlavi script had no means whatever of showing."},{glyph:"𐬅",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER AAN",name:"aan",translit:"ą̇",pronunciation:"The rarer of the two nasal a-vowels.",meaning:"A second nasalised a, distinguished from ą by length or by quality according to which handbook is consulted.",note:"Hoffmann transliterates it ą̇, other editors ą̄, and the Unicode name AAN preserves the reading that takes it as the long member of the pair. That the alphabet's makers heard two nasal a's where the comparative grammar reconstructed since has trouble keeping them apart is a fact about their ears, not about our grammar."},{glyph:"𐬆",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER AE",name:"ae",translit:"ə",pronunciation:"A schwa, the unstressed vowel of English 'about'.",meaning:"Short schwa.",note:"After a, the most frequent vowel on the page: aṣ̌əm, spəṇta, amərətāt̰. Where Vedic has a short a in the same position Avestan often has ə, and the correspondence is one of the levers by which the two languages are set against each other."},{glyph:"𐬇",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER AEE",name:"aee",translit:"ə̄",pronunciation:"A long schwa.",meaning:"Long schwa.",note:"That an alphabet should distinguish a long schwa from a short one is the argument of this whole script compressed into a single pair of signs. The distinction bears directly on the Gathic metre, which is built on syllable count, so the letter is a philological instrument as much as a phonetic one."},{glyph:"𐬈",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER E",name:"e",translit:"e",pronunciation:"Short e as in 'met'.",meaning:"Short e.",note:"Avestan e and o are largely secondary, arising in particular environments rather than inherited from the parent language, which is why they are thin on the page beside a. Their presence in the alphabet records what the priests were saying rather than what the language had once had."},{glyph:"𐬉",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER EE",name:"ee",translit:"ē",pronunciation:"Long e as in French 'été'.",meaning:"Long e.",note:"It is the second element of the diphthong written a + ē — daēuua, daēnā, vaējō — since Avestan spells its diphthongs out as two vowel letters rather than leaving a convention for the reader to supply."},{glyph:"𐬊",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER O",name:"o",translit:"o",pronunciation:"Short o as in 'got'.",meaning:"Short o.",note:"The o of vohu manah, Good Thought — the first of the Bounteous Immortals and, in the tradition's account of what happens after death, the entity that meets the soul."},{glyph:"𐬋",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER OO",name:"oo",translit:"ō",pronunciation:"Long o as in 'more'.",meaning:"Long o.",note:"The ending of a great many nominatives — ahurō, vaējō, frašō — so that the case of a noun is legible at a glance, which is exactly what Book Pahlavi's collapsed letter-shapes had made impossible."},{glyph:"𐬌",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER I",name:"i",translit:"i",pronunciation:"Short i as in 'sit'.",meaning:"Short i.",note:"Doubled, this letter writes the sound we transcribe as y between vowels: what the handbooks print as mainiiu, 'spirit', is spelt in the manuscripts with two i letters, and so are vairiia and tištriia. The doubled transliteration is a description of the page."},{glyph:"𐬍",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER II",name:"ii",translit:"ī",pronunciation:"Long i as in 'machine'.",meaning:"Long i.",note:"Not to be confused with the doubled short i that writes y: this is a single letter for a single long vowel, as in vīdaēuuadāta, the title of the Videvdad, and the distinction between the two is one a transliteration has to work to preserve."},{glyph:"𐬎",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER U",name:"u",translit:"u",pronunciation:"Short u as in 'put'.",meaning:"Short u.",note:"Doubled, u writes the sound we transcribe as v or w between vowels — hauruuatāt̰, frauuaṣ̌i, uruuan — the exact counterpart of the doubled i. Between them the two conventions account for most of the double letters a reader meets."},{glyph:"𐬏",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER UU",name:"uu",translit:"ū",pronunciation:"Long u as in 'rule'.",meaning:"Long u.",note:"It stands in aṣ̌əm vohū, the twelve-word prayer, and in hūxta, 'well-spoken', the middle term of the triad of good thought, good word and good deed."},{glyph:"𐬐",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER KE",name:"ke",translit:"k",pronunciation:"An unaspirated k.",meaning:"The voiceless velar stop.",note:"The first of four velar letters where the Pahlavi script had one workable shape: k, x, x́ and xᵛ are all kept apart here, and it is that fourfold split, more than anything else in the alphabet, that shows what the exercise was for."},{glyph:"𐬑",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER XE",name:"xe",translit:"x",pronunciation:"A voiceless velar fricative, the ch of Scots 'loch'.",meaning:"The voiceless velar fricative.",note:"The x of daxma, the walled enclosure in which the dead are exposed, and of hūxta, 'well-said'. Avestan x answers to Vedic kṣ and to Old Persian x, and the correspondence is regular enough to serve as a test of readings."},{glyph:"𐬒",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER XYE",name:"xye",translit:"x́",pronunciation:"A palatalised x, the fricative made further forward in the mouth.",meaning:"The palatalised velar fricative.",note:"Of restricted occurrence, and its separate existence is easier to record than to justify on grounds of meaning: no pair of Avestan words is told apart by it. It is there because a priest made the sound."},{glyph:"𐬓",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER XVE",name:"xve",translit:"xᵛ",pronunciation:"A labialised x, made with rounded lips, and counted as one sound rather than a cluster.",meaning:"The labialised velar fricative.",note:"It opens xᵛarənah, the glory or fortune that alights on a rightful king and abandons a false one — the word that becomes farr in later Persian, and the subject of the Zamyad Yasht, in which the glory flees Yima three times in the form of a bird."},{glyph:"𐬔",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER GE",name:"ge",translit:"g",pronunciation:"A hard g as in 'give'.",meaning:"The voiced velar stop.",note:"It opens gāθā, 'song', which is Vedic gāthā with hardly a sound altered — the word the tradition uses for the seventeen hymns ascribed to Zarathustra himself and the reason the whole of Old Avestan is often called Gathic."},{glyph:"𐬕",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER GGE",name:"gge",translit:"ġ",pronunciation:"A second g-sound, its exact value not certainly recovered.",meaning:"A second voiced velar, distinguished from g and from γ.",note:"Rare in the texts, and the least well understood letter in the alphabet: the men who cut it knew what it was for and the comparative grammar reconstructed since has struggled to place it. An alphabet that records a sound its own later readers cannot identify is an alphabet transcribing rather than analysing."},{glyph:"𐬖",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER GHE",name:"ghe",translit:"γ",pronunciation:"A voiced velar fricative, the g of Spanish 'lago'.",meaning:"The voiced velar fricative.",note:"The sound in vərəθraγna, name of the yazata of victory, who appears to Zarathustra in Yasht 14 in ten successive forms — among them a wind, a bull with golden horns, a boar, a bird of prey and an armed man."},{glyph:"𐬗",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER CE",name:"ce",translit:"c",pronunciation:"The ch of 'church'.",meaning:"The voiceless palatal affricate.",note:"It opens cinuuatō pərətu, the Bridge of the Separator, which the soul is said to reach on the fourth day after death; the Middle Persian books describe it as broad to the truthful and narrow as a blade's edge to the follower of the Lie."},{glyph:"𐬘",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER JE",name:"je",translit:"j",pronunciation:"The j of 'judge'.",meaning:"The voiced palatal affricate.",note:"The j of druj, the Lie — the power set against aṣ̌a, and the noun from which the tradition's whole vocabulary of wrongdoing is built, so that a wicked man is not called wicked but drəguuaṇt, 'possessed of the Lie'."},{glyph:"𐬙",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER TE",name:"te",translit:"t",pronunciation:"An unaspirated t.",meaning:"The voiceless dental stop.",note:"Ordinary t, written everywhere except at the end of a word, where the alphabet switches to a separate letter for a t that is not released — a division of labour on purely phonetic grounds, with no consequence for meaning at all."},{glyph:"𐬚",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER THE",name:"the",translit:"θ",pronunciation:"The th of 'thin'.",meaning:"The voiceless interdental fricative.",note:"The letter of mąθra, the potent utterance, and of gāθā, the song. Avestan mąθra stands to Vedic mantra as Avestan aṣ̌a stands to Vedic ṛta: the same word, arrived at by different sound-laws from a common ancestor."},{glyph:"𐬛",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER DE",name:"de",translit:"d",pronunciation:"An unaspirated d.",meaning:"The voiced dental stop.",note:"It opens daēnā — vision, conscience, and eventually 'religion' — and daēuua, the class of beings the tradition rejects; two of the most consequential nouns in the corpus begin with the same three letters."},{glyph:"𐬜",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER DHE",name:"dhe",translit:"δ",pronunciation:"The th of 'this'.",meaning:"The voiced interdental fricative.",note:"It stands between vowels where d stands elsewhere. The difference is positional and automatic — no Avestan word means one thing with d and another with δ — and the alphabet writes it anyway, which is the clearest single statement of its purpose."},{glyph:"𐬝",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER TTE",name:"tte",translit:"t̰",pronunciation:"A t with no audible release, at the end of a word.",meaning:"The unreleased final t.",note:"Written in yat̰, in hauruuatāt̰ and in amərətāt̰, and nowhere else but word-end. A letter spent on an allophone is a letter spent on a manner of speaking, and this is the sign most often cited as proof that the alphabet was made to fix a recitation rather than a text."},{glyph:"𐬞",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER PE",name:"pe",translit:"p",pronunciation:"An unaspirated p.",meaning:"The voiceless labial stop.",note:"It opens pairikā, the female demon of the texts, whose name survives as Persian parī and entered English in the eighteenth century as 'peri'."},{glyph:"𐬟",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER FE",name:"fe",translit:"f",pronunciation:"An f.",meaning:"The voiceless labial fricative.",note:"The f of frauuaṣ̌i, the pre-existent soul, and of frašō.kərəti, the Making Wonderful at the end of time. Both begin with the prefix that Vedic writes pra-: Avestan turns p to f before r, and the two words are among the plainest illustrations of the rule."},{glyph:"𐬠",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER BE",name:"be",translit:"b",pronunciation:"A b.",meaning:"The voiced labial stop.",note:"It opens barəsman, the bundle of twigs held by the officiating priest, which in Parsi practice today is a bundle of metal wires, and barəšnu, from which the nine-night purification takes its name."},{glyph:"𐬡",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER BHE",name:"bhe",translit:"β",pronunciation:"A voiced bilabial fricative, a v made with both lips.",meaning:"The voiced labial fricative.",note:"It stands to b as δ stands to d — a fricative written where the stop would misrepresent the sound — and appears in aiβi, 'about, around', the preverb that opens a good many compound names in the ritual texts."},{glyph:"𐬢",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER NGE",name:"nge",translit:"ŋ",pronunciation:"The ng of 'sing'.",meaning:"The velar nasal.",note:"A letter, not a digraph. It stands in aŋra mainiiu, the Destructive Spirit, and in vaŋhu, 'good' — and it is the first of three velar nasals, where most alphabets manage without even one."},{glyph:"𐬣",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER NGYE",name:"ngye",translit:"ŋ́",pronunciation:"A palatalised velar nasal.",meaning:"The palatalised velar nasal.",note:"Nothing in Avestan morphology requires this letter to be told from its neighbours, and a reader who knew the language would never have needed it distinguished. The three ŋ letters are among the clearest evidence that the alphabet was assembled by ear."},{glyph:"𐬤",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER NGVE",name:"ngve",translit:"ŋᵛ",pronunciation:"A labialised velar nasal, made with rounded lips.",meaning:"The labialised velar nasal.",note:"The third of the velar nasals, and the rarest. Its existence completes a pattern the alphabet keeps everywhere else — plain, palatalised and labialised — applied here to a sound that in most languages goes unnoticed altogether."},{glyph:"𐬥",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER NE",name:"ne",translit:"n",pronunciation:"An n.",meaning:"The dental nasal.",note:"Ordinary n. It is the letter of nasu, the corpse-matter and the demoness of it, which the Videvdad says rushes upon a body from the north in the form of a fly."},{glyph:"𐬦",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER NYE",name:"nye",translit:"ń",pronunciation:"A palatal n, the ñ of Spanish 'año'.",meaning:"The palatal nasal.",note:"A positional variant of n given its own sign, on the same principle as δ beside d and t̰ beside t: what the alphabet records is not the phoneme but the sound made."},{glyph:"𐬧",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER NNE",name:"nne",translit:"ṇ",pronunciation:"The nasal as pronounced before a following consonant.",meaning:"The nasal of consonant clusters.",note:"Written in spəṇta, 'bounteous', and in saošiiaṇt, the one who will bring benefit. A plain n would have served any reader who knew the words; this letter serves the one who is listening."},{glyph:"𐬨",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER ME",name:"me",translit:"m",pronunciation:"An m.",meaning:"The labial nasal.",note:"It opens mazdā, 'Wisdom', the second element of the name of God, and miθra, 'contract' — and, in the Gathas, mąθra, the utterance by which the poet says he does his work."},{glyph:"𐬩",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER HME",name:"hme",translit:"m̨",pronunciation:"The m of the group hm, devoiced by the breath before it.",meaning:"A second m, confined to the cluster written hm.",note:"Transliterated m̨ by some editors and m̥ by others. The alphabet has two letters for m and spends the second on a single consonant cluster; a script economising on signs would not have done it, and this one was not economising."},{glyph:"𐬪",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER YYE",name:"yye",translit:"ẏ",pronunciation:"A y-sound.",meaning:"One of two letters for y.",note:"Avestan has two separate letters for y, besides the doubled i that writes the same sound between vowels — three ways of putting a y on the page. Which is used where is governed by position and by the habits of particular manuscript families, and editors have argued the distribution rather than settled it."},{glyph:"𐬫",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER YE",name:"ye",translit:"y",pronunciation:"The y of 'yes'.",meaning:"The second and commoner letter for y.",note:"It opens yasna, the act of worship and the name of the chief liturgy, and yazata, 'worthy of worship', the class of beings to whom the Yashts are addressed. Both derive from the same verb, yaz-, 'to sacrifice, to worship'."},{glyph:"𐬬",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER VE",name:"ve",translit:"v",pronunciation:"A v or w.",meaning:"The labial semivowel or fricative.",note:"Written where the sound begins a word or a syllable, while between vowels the identical sound is written with two u letters — so vohu begins with this letter and hauruuatāt̰, for all its w, does not contain it."},{glyph:"𐬭",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER RE",name:"re",translit:"r",pronunciation:"A tapped or trilled r.",meaning:"The liquid r.",note:"Avestan r does the work of both r and l: where Vedic and Old Persian have l, Iranian regularly has r, and the language managed without any l at all until a letter for one was added for other purposes."},{glyph:"𐬮",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER LE",name:"le",translit:"l",pronunciation:"An l.",meaning:"The lateral l — a sound Avestan itself does not have.",note:"The odd letter out, and a later addition. It was needed for Pazand, in which the Avestan alphabet was pressed into service to write Middle Persian phonetically and so escape the ambiguity of the Pahlavi script, and for foreign words. Whether it is admitted is why the alphabet is given as fifty-three letters in one handbook and fifty-four in the next.",archaic:!0},{glyph:"𐬯",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER SE",name:"se",translit:"s",pronunciation:"An unvoiced s.",meaning:"The voiceless sibilant.",note:"It stands in yasna, the act of worship, which is the same word as Vedic yajña; the ceremony the two traditions name with it is in both the offering by which the world is kept in being."},{glyph:"𐬰",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER ZE",name:"ze",translit:"z",pronunciation:"A voiced s, as in 'zeal'.",meaning:"The voiced sibilant.",note:"It opens zaotar, the officiating priest, which is the same word as Vedic hotar, the pourer of the offering — so that the two priesthoods, having parted before either literature existed, carry one title between them."},{glyph:"𐬱",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER SHE",name:"she",translit:"š",pronunciation:"The sh of 'ship'.",meaning:"The plain postalveolar sibilant.",note:"The first of four sibilants beyond s and z. It is the š of vahišta, 'best', in the name Aṣ̌a Vahišta, and of tištriia, the star Sirius, whose Yasht makes him a white stallion contending with the demon of drought."},{glyph:"𐬲",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER ZHE",name:"zhe",translit:"ž",pronunciation:"The s of 'measure'.",meaning:"The voiced postalveolar sibilant.",note:"The ž of aži dahāka, the three-mouthed dragon of Yashts 9 and 19, bound by Θraētaona and, in the Middle Persian books, confined under Mount Damāvand until the end — the same figure who arrives in Firdausi's Shahnameh a thousand years later as Zahhāk, with serpents growing from his shoulders."},{glyph:"𐬳",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER SHYE",name:"shye",translit:"š́",pronunciation:"A palatalised sh.",meaning:"The palatalised postalveolar sibilant.",note:"It opens š́iiaoθna, 'action' — the third term of the triad of thought, word and deed on which Zoroastrian ethics is built, and a word the Gathas use for what a person will be judged by."},{glyph:"𐬴",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER SSHE",name:"sshe",translit:"ṣ̌",pronunciation:"A retroflex sh, the tongue drawn back.",meaning:"The retroflex sibilant, arising from an older cluster rt.",note:"The most consequential letter in the alphabet, because it writes the ṣ̌ of aṣ̌a, Truth. The sound descends from an older rt, so that Avestan aṣ̌a, Vedic ṛta and the arta- of Old Persian royal names are one and the same noun: Artaxerxes and the first word of Zoroastrian ethics are built on it alike. The same rule gives frauuaṣ̌i its ṣ̌."},{glyph:"𐬵",unicodeName:"AVESTAN LETTER HE",name:"he",translit:"h",pronunciation:"An h.",meaning:"The glottal fricative.",note:"It opens haoma, the plant pressed in the Yasna. Avestan h answers regularly to Vedic s — haoma to soma, hapta to sapta, hindu to sindhu — and that single correspondence is among the sharpest tests for telling an Iranian word from an Indic one."},{glyph:"𐬹",unicodeName:"AVESTAN ABBREVIATION MARK",name:"abbreviation mark",translit:"—",pronunciation:"None; a mark, not a letter.",meaning:"The sign of an abbreviated formula.",note:"It stands where a formula recurring through the liturgy has been cut short on the page, the scribe writing its opening words and leaving the rest to the reciter. It is a small piece of evidence about what these books were for: they were not made to be read by anyone who did not already know the text by heart."},{glyph:"𐬼",unicodeName:"LARGE TWO DOTS OVER ONE DOT PUNCTUATION",name:"large two dots over one dot",translit:"—",pronunciation:"None; a mark of division.",meaning:"A section divider.",note:"Avestan divides every word from the next with a single dot; larger arrangements of dots and rings, of which Unicode encodes six, divide verses and sections. The system is graded by size and by the count of the dots rather than by their shape, and the manuscripts are not consistent in applying it."}],divineNames:[{native:"𐬀𐬵𐬎𐬭𐬀 𐬨𐬀𐬰𐬛𐬁",translit:"ahura mazdā",english:"Ahura Mazdā, the Wise Lord",meaning:"'Lord' and 'Wisdom' — two nouns, not a compound, and in the Gathas each is declined on its own and either may come first.",context:"The Gathas throughout; the Yasna is offered to him; Middle Persian Ohrmazd, modern Persian Hormozd.",note:"That the name is still two words in Old Avestan, taking case endings separately and appearing as often as mazdā ahura, makes the later single name Ohrmazd a linguistic event as well as a theological one. The Achaemenid inscriptions name Auramazdā in Old Persian from Darius onward, some three centuries before any Zoroastrian text was written down; what relation the kings' god bears to the god of the Gathas is among the oldest questions in the field and is not settled."},{native:"𐬯𐬞𐬆𐬧𐬙𐬀 𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬥𐬌𐬌𐬎",translit:"spəṇta mainiiu",english:"the Bounteous Spirit",meaning:"'Spirit' with the adjective spəṇta, which the handbooks render 'holy', 'bounteous' or 'incremental' and which no English word fits.",context:"Yasna 30.3, where two spirits are called twins; Yasna 45.2; the third of the Gathic groups is named from him.",note:"Whether the Bounteous Spirit is Ahura Mazdā himself or a distinct entity through whom he acts divides readers of the Gathas. Younger Avestan and the Pahlavi books identify the two, so that Ohrmazd stands directly against Ahriman; several readers of the older text hold instead that it sets the Bounteous and the Destructive Spirits as a pair beneath Ahura Mazdā, which would make the earliest Zoroastrian dualism a division inside the creation rather than at the top of it."},{native:"𐬀𐬢𐬭𐬀 𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬥𐬌𐬌𐬎",translit:"aŋra mainiiu",english:"the Destructive Spirit, Ahriman",meaning:"'The spirit that harms' — aŋra is the adjective of injury, not a proper name.",context:"Chiefly Younger Avestan, where the compound is fixed; Yasna 45.2 is the passage usually cited for earlier use. Middle Persian Ahriman.",note:"The Bundahishn, the Pahlavi book that systematises the cosmology, opens with Ohrmazd in the light above and Ahriman in the darkness below, separated by the void; Ohrmazd recites the Ahuna Vairya and Ahriman falls back stupefied for three thousand years. The whole of world history in that account is the middle stretch of a twelve-thousand-year scheme in which the two are mixed, and which ends with the mixture undone."},{native:"𐬀𐬴𐬀 𐬬𐬀𐬵𐬌𐬱𐬙𐬀",translit:"aṣ̌a vahišta",english:"Best Truth",meaning:"Truth, right order, the way things properly are — with the superlative 'best'.",context:"The Gathas; the Amesha Spenta whose creation is fire, and after whom the third day of the month and the month Ardibehesht are named.",note:"Aṣ̌a is Vedic ṛta and Old Persian arta, and the word resists translation in all three languages: it is at once cosmic order, ritual correctness and moral truth, and the tradition's central opposition is not good against evil but aṣ̌a against druj, order against the Lie."},{native:"𐬬𐬊𐬵𐬎 𐬨𐬀𐬥𐬀𐬵",translit:"vohu manah",english:"Good Thought",meaning:"'Good mind' or 'good thought' — a mental disposition raised to a person.",context:"The Gathas, where Zarathustra says he came to him first; the Amesha Spenta whose creation is cattle. Middle Persian Bahman.",note:"The Bounteous Immortals are abstractions given a life of their own: Good Thought, Best Truth, Desirable Dominion, Bounteous Devotion, Wholeness and Immortality. The collective term aməša spəṇta belongs to Younger Avestan; the Gathas name the six individually and never as a college, which is one reason for holding the sixfold scheme to be a later ordering of older material."},{native:"𐬑𐬱𐬀𐬚𐬭𐬀 𐬬𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬀",translit:"xšaθra vairiia",english:"Desirable Dominion",meaning:"'Power that is to be chosen' — sovereignty as it ought to be exercised rather than as it is.",context:"The Gathas; the Amesha Spenta whose creations are the sky and metal. Middle Persian Shahrevar.",note:"Its attachment to metal governs the eschatology of the Pahlavi books, in which the mountains are melted at the end and the river of molten metal runs over the earth, feeling to the truthful like warm milk and to the wicked like what it is."},{native:"𐬯𐬞𐬆𐬧𐬙𐬁 𐬁𐬭𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌",translit:"spəṇtā ārmaiti",english:"Bounteous Right-Mindedness",meaning:"Devotion, right-mindedness, the settled disposition of the loyal — the word covers piety and fitting thought together.",context:"The Gathas; the Amesha Spenta whose creation is the earth. Middle Persian Spandarmad; the month Esfand.",note:"She is feminine, and the tradition identifies her with the earth itself, so that agriculture, care of the ground and devotion are one continuous obligation — which is why the Videvdad's praise of the man who tills is not a digression from its purity law but its other half."},{native:"𐬵𐬀𐬎𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬙𐬁𐬝",translit:"hauruuatāt̰",english:"Wholeness, Health",meaning:"'Entirety' — an abstract noun in -tāt̰, the same suffix as Latin -tas.",context:"The Gathas; the Amesha Spenta whose creation is the waters. Middle Persian Hordad; the month Khordad.",note:"Hauruuatāt̰ and Amərətāt̰ are a fixed pair and answer to Vedic sarvatāt- and amṛtatva-. An old proposal identifies them with Hārūt and Mārūt, the two angels of Qur'an 2:102; it is repeated in the handbooks more often than it is demonstrated, and the phonetic steps have never been shown."},{native:"𐬀𐬨𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬙𐬁𐬝",translit:"amərətāt̰",english:"Immortality",meaning:"'Not-dying', the a- privative on the root mar-, 'to die'.",context:"The Gathas; the Amesha Spenta whose creation is the plants. Middle Persian Amurdad; the month Mordad.",note:"The same formation stands in Vedic amṛta and in Greek ambrosia, so that the word for the last of the Bounteous Immortals is cognate with the food of the Olympians. In the calendar the six give their names to six of the twelve months, which is how an abstraction ends up governing a farmer's year."},{native:"𐬯𐬭𐬀𐬊𐬱𐬀",translit:"sraoša",english:"Sraosha, Hearkening",meaning:"'Listening, obedience' — the act of hearing what is said and doing it.",context:"Yasna 57, the Srosh Yasht, one of the longest chapters of the liturgy; invoked at every performance of the rite. Middle Persian Srōsh.",note:"He is described as guarding the creation at night with a raised weapon, and the tradition assigns him the protection of the soul during the three days it is said to remain near the body — which is why the prayers of those three days are named for him and why he is the yazata most often addressed in the funerary offices."},{native:"𐬨𐬌𐬚𐬭𐬀",translit:"miθra",english:"Mithra",meaning:"'Contract, covenant' — an ordinary noun, and the being is the contract personified.",context:"Yasht 10, the Mihr Yasht, among the longest of the Yashts; his name is the sixteenth day of the month and the month Mehr.",note:"He is described as having a thousand ears and ten thousand eyes, sleepless, watching whether men keep their agreements. The relation between this figure and the Mithras of the Roman mystery cult is one of the field's long arguments: Franz Cumont's derivation of the Roman cult straight from Iranian religion has been largely abandoned since the 1970s, and what remains uncontested is a shared name."},{native:"𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬛𐬎𐬎𐬍 𐬯𐬏𐬭𐬁 𐬀𐬥𐬁𐬵𐬌𐬙𐬁",translit:"arəduuī sūrā anāhitā",english:"Aredvi Sura Anahita",meaning:"'The moist, the strong, the undefiled' — three epithets, of which the last has become the name.",context:"Yasht 5, the Aban Yasht, addressed to the waters; her day is the tenth of the month.",note:"The Yasht describes her driving a chariot drawn by four horses — wind, rain, cloud and sleet — and wearing a cloak of beaver skins. Artaxerxes II, reigning in the first half of the fourth century BCE, is the first Achaemenid to name Anāhitā and Mithra beside Ahuramazdā in his inscriptions, and that break in three generations of royal formula is one of the few hard dates anywhere near this material."},{native:"𐬬𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬚𐬭𐬀𐬖𐬥𐬀",translit:"vərəθraγna",english:"Verethragna, Victory",meaning:"'The smashing of resistance' — an abstract, not an agent noun.",context:"Yasht 14, the Bahram Yasht. Middle Persian Wahrām, whence the planet Mars in Persian and the name of the highest grade of consecrated fire.",note:"The Vedic epithet vṛtrahan- belongs to Indra, the dragon-slayer, while the Iranian word remains an abstraction that manifests in ten forms; the classic treatment of the divergence is Benveniste and Renou's Vṛtra et Vṛθragna of 1934, and whether an Indo-Iranian dragon-fight lies behind both, or the Vedic figure is a secondary personification, is still argued."},{native:"𐬙𐬌𐬱𐬙𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬀",translit:"tištriia",english:"Tishtrya, Sirius",meaning:"The star Sirius, identified as such by the tradition and by the astronomy.",context:"Yasht 8, the Tir Yasht; his day is the thirteenth of the month, and the summer water-festival Tiragan is named from the Middle Persian form.",note:"The Yasht makes him a white stallion with golden ears who contends at the shore of the mythic sea Vourukasha with Apaoša, the demon of drought, in the form of a black horse; he is beaten at first for want of offerings, and prevails when he is worshipped. The rains follow the outcome."},{native:"𐬁𐬙𐬀𐬭",translit:"ātar",english:"Fire",meaning:"Fire, and the yazata of it, called in the texts the son of Ahura Mazdā.",context:"The Atash Nyayesh; present at every performance of the Yasna. Middle Persian ādur, modern Persian ātaš.",note:"Zoroastrians keep three grades of consecrated fire — the Dadgah, the Adaran, and the Atash Behram, which is enthroned after an elaborate consecration drawing fire from many sources. The Iranshah fire at Udvada in Gujarat is the one the Parsi community holds to have been carried with it from Iran. Its members describe fire as that toward which prayer is directed rather than its object, and say so often enough to indicate how frequently the point has had to be made."},{native:"𐬯𐬀𐬊𐬱𐬌𐬌𐬀𐬧𐬙",translit:"saošiiaṇt",english:"Saoshyant, the one who will bring benefit",meaning:"A future participle of the verb sav-, 'to benefit, to strengthen' — 'he who will make strong'.",context:"The Gathas, mostly in the plural; Yasht 13 and Yasht 19; the Pahlavi apocalyptic texts, where the figure becomes singular and final.",note:"In the Gathas the word is close to a common noun for those who will set things right, and Zarathustra can be counted among them. The developed doctrine — three saviours born at the ends of successive millennia from seed of Zarathustra preserved in a lake, the last of them, Astvat-ərəta, raising the dead and completing the frašō.kərəti — belongs to the Younger Avestan and Middle Persian texts, and the distance between the two usages is one of the standard illustrations of how much the tradition developed after its oldest layer."}],lexicon:[{native:"𐬫𐬀𐬯𐬥𐬀",translit:"yasna",english:"worship, sacrifice; the chief liturgy",domain:"ritual",definition:"The act of worship, and the name of the seventy-two-chapter ceremony that is the tradition's central rite. It is performed today by two priests, the zōt and the rāspī, in a consecrated precinct, and takes some two hours; the word is Vedic yajña, the same noun in the same sense. The seventy-two threads of the kusti, the cord Zoroastrians wear, are traditionally counted against the seventy-two chapters."},{native:"𐬨𐬀𐬥𐬚𐬭𐬀",translit:"mąθra",english:"sacred utterance",domain:"ritual",definition:"A formulated word of power — Vedic mantra, the same noun. The Gathas call Zarathustra's own speech mąθra, and the compound mąθra spəṇta names the utterance considered as an instrument that acts. The tradition's regard for the exact sound of a recitation, and so for the alphabet made to preserve it, follows from this word rather than from any theory of scripture."},{native:"𐬵𐬀𐬊𐬨𐬀",translit:"haoma",english:"haoma, the pressed plant",domain:"ritual",definition:"The plant pounded in a mortar and its juice, offered and consumed in the Yasna; Vedic soma. Its ancient identity is unsettled: R. Gordon Wasson argued in 1968 for the fly agaric, others for wild rue, and ephedra is the candidate most favoured now, partly because ephedra is what living Iranian and Parsi priests use. Yasna 9 to 11, the Hom Yasht, has the plant speak in its own voice."},{native:"𐬠𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬯𐬨𐬀𐬥",translit:"barəsman",english:"the ritual bundle",domain:"ritual",definition:"A bundle of twigs held by the officiating priest during the ceremony and laid on its stand. In Parsi practice the twigs have long been replaced by metal wires, a substitution the community itself dates and discusses; the object is one of the few pieces of ancient Iranian ritual furniture still in daily use."},{native:"𐬰𐬀𐬊𐬙𐬀𐬭",translit:"zaotar",english:"the officiating priest",domain:"priesthood",definition:"The priest who performs the Yasna, literally 'the pourer'; Vedic hotar. Middle Persian zōt, and the title of the senior of the two priests at a modern performance. The Gathas call Zarathustra himself a zaotar, which is one of the arguments for reading them as compositions of a working priest rather than of a reformer standing outside the cult."},{native:"𐬀𐬚𐬭𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬥",translit:"āθrauuan",english:"priest",domain:"priesthood",definition:"The general Avestan word for a priest, and the first of the social classes in the texts. Whether it contains ātar, 'fire', or answers rather to Vedic atharvan- is disputed and has been since the nineteenth century; the Middle Persian descendant āsrōn shows the word surviving the argument."},{native:"𐬀𐬉𐬚𐬭𐬀𐬞𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌",translit:"aēθrapaiti",english:"teaching priest",domain:"priesthood",definition:"'Master of pupils', the priest who instructs. The word descends through Middle Persian ērbad to the Parsi title ervad, borne today by a priest who has taken the first initiation — so that a title for a teacher of memorised texts has survived, in continuous use, the entire history of the written tradition."},{native:"𐬀𐬴𐬀",translit:"aṣ̌a",english:"truth, right order",domain:"cosmology",definition:"The governing term of Zoroastrian ethics and cosmology at once: the order by which things are rightly arranged, the correctness of a rite, and truth in speech. Vedic ṛta and Old Persian arta are the same word. It has no English equivalent, and translators who choose 'truth' and translators who choose 'righteousness' are each giving up half of it."},{native:"𐬛𐬭𐬎𐬘",translit:"druj",english:"the Lie",domain:"cosmology",definition:"Deceit, falsehood, and the active principle opposed to aṣ̌a. The Gathas divide humanity into the aṣ̌auuan, possessed of truth, and the drəguuaṇt, possessed of the Lie, and the same term is used of Angra Mainyu's whole creation. The Old Persian of Darius's Behistun inscription uses the cognate drauga for the pretenders he overthrew, in a text that never mentions Zarathustra."},{native:"𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬎𐬎𐬀",translit:"daēuua",english:"daeva, false god, demon",domain:"cosmology",definition:"A class of beings the tradition rejects. The word is Indo-Iranian and means simply 'god' — Vedic deva, Latin deus — so the Iranian usage records an inversion: what the Indic branch worships, this one repudiates, while its word for lord, ahura, answers to the Vedic asura, which travels the opposite way. Xerxes' Old Persian inscription at Persepolis records the destruction of a place where daivas had been worshipped; where that was, and whether the act was Zoroastrian, is argued and unresolved."},{native:"𐬫𐬀𐬰𐬀𐬙𐬀",translit:"yazata",english:"one worthy of worship",domain:"cosmology",definition:"The class of divine beings below Ahura Mazdā and the Amesha Spentas — Mithra, Anahita, Sraosha, Tishtrya, Verethragna and the rest — to whom the twenty-one Yashts are addressed. Middle Persian yazad. Thirty of them give their names to the thirty days of the Zoroastrian month, so that the calendar itself is a list of the powers."},{native:"𐬓𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬥𐬀𐬵",translit:"xᵛarənah",english:"glory, fortune",domain:"cosmology",definition:"The radiant fortune that attaches to kings, to priests and to the coming saviours. Yasht 19 describes it fleeing Yima three times in the shape of a bird when he takes a lie into his mouth. It becomes farr in later Persian and is a working term of Iranian kingship for another thousand years; the winged figure of Achaemenid reliefs has been read as the royal xᵛarənah, as Ahura Mazdā and as a fravashi, and none of the three has carried the field."},{native:"𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬀𐬥𐬆𐬨 𐬬𐬀𐬉𐬘𐬋",translit:"airiianəm vaējō",english:"Airyanem Vaejah, the Aryan expanse",domain:"cosmology",definition:"The first of the sixteen lands in the opening chapter of the Videvdad, and the tradition's homeland, where winter is said to last ten months. Its location has been placed in Chorasmia by W. B. Henning, in Sistan by Gherardo Gnoli, and in Bactria and the Oxus region by others; the argument turns on the order of the sixteen lands, which may be a route, a rough geography, or neither."},{native:"𐬀𐬲𐬌 𐬛𐬀𐬵𐬁𐬐𐬀",translit:"aži dahāka",english:"Azhi Dahaka, the dragon",domain:"cosmology",definition:"The three-mouthed, three-headed, six-eyed serpent of Yashts 9 and 19, smitten by Θraētaona; the Middle Persian books have him bound beneath Mount Damāvand until the end of the world, when he breaks loose. In Firdausi's Shahnameh, some two centuries after those books, he is Zahhāk, a tyrant with serpents growing from his shoulders, and the dragon has become a man."},{native:"𐬗𐬌𐬥𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬙𐬋 𐬞𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬙𐬎",translit:"cinuuatō pərətu",english:"the Bridge of the Separator",domain:"eschatology",definition:"The crossing the soul is said to reach at the dawn of the fourth day after death. The Gathas already name it; the Middle Persian books describe it widening for the truthful and narrowing to the edge of a blade for the follower of the Lie, with the soul's own deeds weighed at the near end."},{native:"𐬟𐬭𐬀𐬱𐬋 𐬐𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬙𐬌",translit:"frašō.kərəti",english:"the Making Wonderful",domain:"eschatology",definition:"The final restoration of the world to the state it was made in: the dead raised, the mountains melted into a river of metal through which all pass, the Lie undone and time itself brought to a stop. Middle Persian frašgird. The compound is written with a dot between its members, as Avestan writes all compounds, and the whole eschatology of the tradition is contained in these two words."},{native:"𐬟𐬭𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬴𐬌",translit:"frauuaṣ̌i",english:"fravashi",domain:"soul",definition:"The pre-existent and immortal element of a person, distinct from the soul and older than the body; the fravashis of the dead, of the living and of the unborn are all invoked together. Yasht 13, the longest of the Yashts, is addressed to them, and the Fravardigan days at the year's end are kept for them still, when families visit the fire temples and the dead are named."},{native:"𐬎𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬥",translit:"uruuan",english:"soul",domain:"soul",definition:"The soul proper, which departs at death and is judged; Middle Persian ruwān. It is distinguished from the fravashi, which does not undergo judgement, and the two are named separately in the same breath in the liturgies — a division of the person that has no equivalent in the Abrahamic accounts."},{native:"𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬥𐬁",translit:"daēnā",english:"vision, conscience, religion",domain:"soul",definition:"What a person sees and is; the inner disposition, and by extension the religion itself. Middle Persian dēn, modern Persian din. In the Hadhokht Nask the daēnā meets the soul after death as a young woman, beautiful in proportion to the life lived and hideous in proportion to it. Whether Arabic dīn is borrowed from the Iranian word or belongs to a Semitic root meaning 'judgement' has been argued both ways for a century."},{native:"𐬠𐬀𐬊𐬛𐬀𐬵",translit:"baodah",english:"consciousness, perception",domain:"soul",definition:"The faculty of awareness, cognate with Greek peuthomai and with the participle behind the title Buddha. Yasht 13 lists five constituents of a person — ahu, daēnā, baodah, uruuan and frauuaṣ̌i — and the list is invoked entire in the liturgy, so that the tradition's anthropology is recited rather than argued."},{native:"𐬔𐬁𐬚𐬁",translit:"gāθā",english:"song, hymn",domain:"text",definition:"Vedic gāthā, and in the plural the seventeen hymns ascribed to Zarathustra, arranged in five groups by their metres and set at the heart of the Yasna. They are the oldest Avestan and among the most difficult texts in any Indo-European language: their vocabulary is small, their syntax compressed, and no two translations of a stanza agree."},{native:"𐬯𐬙𐬀𐬊𐬙𐬀 𐬫𐬈𐬯𐬥𐬌𐬌𐬀",translit:"staota yesniia",english:"'the praises worthy of worship'",domain:"text",definition:"The tradition's own name for the Old Avestan core of the Yasna — the Gathas, the prose Yasna Haptaŋhāiti and the great short prayers — as against the Younger Avestan material assembled around it. The distinction is the tradition's, not a modern editorial one, and it is the closest thing in the corpus to a statement of what its oldest layer is."},{native:"𐬬𐬍𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬛𐬁𐬙𐬀",translit:"vīdaēuuadāta",english:"the Videvdad, 'the law given against the daevas'",domain:"text",definition:"Twenty-two chapters of purity law, penance and myth, including the sixteen lands, the regulations for the disposal of the dead, the treatment of dogs and the nine-night purification. It is the only book of the twenty-one nasks of the Sasanian collection preserved entire, and its language is late and in places evidently imperfectly understood by its own compilers. Its authority has been contested within the community since the nineteenth-century reform movements, some holding it a priestly accretion; it continues to be recited in the night ceremony that bears its name."},{native:"𐬀𐬴𐬆𐬨 𐬬𐬊𐬵𐬏",translit:"aṣ̌əm vohū",english:"the Ashem Vohu prayer",domain:"text",definition:"Twelve words in Old Avestan on the value of truth, and the shortest and most often recited of Zoroastrian prayers. Its oldest witness is not an Avestan manuscript at all: a fragment written in Sogdian script, recovered from Dunhuang and now in the British Library, preserves the prayer in a pronunciation older than the manuscript tradition. It is usually dated to about the ninth century and was published by Ilya Gershevitch."},{native:"𐬫𐬀𐬚𐬁 𐬀𐬵𐬏 𐬬𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬋",translit:"yaθā ahū vairiiō",english:"the Ahuna Vairya prayer",domain:"text",definition:"Twenty-one words in Old Avestan, the tradition's most potent formula, whose sense is disputed line by line. The twenty-one words were counted against the twenty-one nasks of the Sasanian Avesta, and the Bundahishn has Ohrmazd utter the prayer before creation to rout Ahriman — a text of twenty-one words made to stand for a library and for the first act of the world."},{native:"𐬫𐬁𐬙𐬎",translit:"yātu",english:"sorcery, sorcerer",domain:"magic",definition:"The practitioner of illicit ritual and the practice itself, denounced throughout the Younger Avestan texts and paired conventionally with the pairikā. As elsewhere, the word is applied to others and never claimed: the same acts of formula and offering are yasna when performed by a priest and yātu when performed against the order the priest serves."},{native:"𐬞𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬐𐬁",translit:"pairikā",english:"pairika, female demon",domain:"magic",definition:"A female malign being who deceives and seduces, driven off in the same formulae as the sorcerer. The word becomes Middle Persian parīg and modern Persian parī, by which time she has softened into the beautiful and ambiguous spirit of Persian poetry, and it entered English in the eighteenth century as 'peri'."},{native:"𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬎",translit:"nasu",english:"corpse-matter; the corpse demon",domain:"purity",definition:"Dead matter and the demoness of it. The Videvdad describes her rushing upon a body from the north in the form of a fly the moment life leaves it, and its entire purity regime follows from that account: the dead may not be given to fire, water or earth, all of which are good creations, and are exposed instead."},{native:"𐬛𐬀𐬑𐬨𐬀",translit:"daxma",english:"structure of exposure",domain:"purity",definition:"The walled enclosure on which the dead are exposed. The English phrase 'tower of silence' is a nineteenth-century coinage and translates nothing. The practice continues at Doongerwadi in Mumbai and in Iranian communities where it has not been prohibited; it has been under acute strain since the 1990s, when the vulture population of the Indian subcontinent collapsed after the veterinary use of diclofenac, and solar concentrators have been installed at some sites in response."},{native:"𐬠𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬱𐬥𐬎",translit:"barəšnu",english:"the crown of the head; the great purification",domain:"purity",definition:"An Avestan noun for the top of the head, from which the Middle Persian name of the barəšnūm ī nō šab, the nine-night purification, is taken. The rite is set out in the Videvdad, involves successive washings and a period of seclusion, and is still undergone by candidates for the priesthood before their higher initiation."},{native:"𐬵𐬁𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬥𐬌",translit:"hāuuani",english:"the morning watch",domain:"time",definition:"The first of the five divisions of the day, from sunrise to noon, at which the Yasna is normally performed. Its name is taken from the hāuuana, the mortar in which the haoma is pounded: the day's first period is named after a piece of ritual equipment, which is a fair statement of how this calendar was built."},{native:"𐬰𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬥",translit:"zruuan",english:"time",domain:"time",definition:"Time, and in a Sasanian development the figure of Zurvan, from whom Ohrmazd and Ahriman are said to be born as twins — a scheme that would place a neutral principle above the two spirits. It is known almost entirely from hostile outside report, the fullest being that of the fifth-century Armenian Eznik of Kolb. R. C. Zaehner argued that Zurvanism was the dominant form of Sasanian religion; later scholarship has generally reduced it to one current among others, and the sources do not permit a decision."},{native:"𐬵𐬎𐬨𐬀𐬙𐬀 𐬵𐬏𐬑𐬙𐬀 𐬵𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬭𐬱𐬙𐬀",translit:"humata hūxta huuaršta",english:"good thoughts, good words, good deeds",domain:"ethics",definition:"The triad on which the tradition's ethics rests, three past participles with the prefix hu-, 'well'. It is recited in the liturgy and is inscribed over the doorways of fire temples today, in Gujarat, in Iran and in the diaspora, usually in the Avestan script — one of the few contexts in which these letters are still cut in stone."}]};export{e as default};
